WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:08.770
ZOS Unix is made up of a system and application services.

2
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.810
There's a system application programming interface,

3
00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:15.050
or a system API, which is a set of C language functions

4
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.890
and the operating system interfaces that support them.

5
00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:23.290
Some services are also accessible through a simple language.

6
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.930
There are shell and utility interactive interfaces

7
00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:29.810
to ZOS Unix services.

8
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.650
A hierarchical file system is a byte-oriented file system

9
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.730
familiar to Unix programmers,

10
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.930
and DBX, or Debugger for Unix,

11
00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:40.690
is an interactive tool for debugging

12
00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:45.690
C and C++ language applications that use ZOS Unix services.

13
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.330
The picture shown here shows how ZOS Unix,

14
00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.130
the shell interface, and the API

15
00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:58.410
relate to the rest of the ZOS operating system.

16
00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.410
Here we see the API interface and shell interface

17
00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.490
leading into language environments,

18
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.610
which executes the shell and utilities.

19
00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.850
BCP is the base control program.

20
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.650
We have multiple file systems here,

21
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:14.850
hierarchical file system, ZOS file system,

22
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.890
and network file system.

23
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.450
Support is going away for the HFS data structure

24
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.250
used by the ZOS Unix environment,

25
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.650
so you want to migrate your HFS to ZFS

26
00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:32.430
and enjoy the performance gains that come from doing that.

27
00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:36.030
The Unix file system itself is hierarchical

28
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.910
and byte-oriented.

29
00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.830
Finding a file in this file system

30
00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.630
is done by searching a directory or a series of directories.

31
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.430
There's no concept of like a ZOS catalog

32
00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.430
that points directly to a file.

33
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.710
A path name identifies a file

34
00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.310
and consists of directory names and a file name.

35
00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:56.910
A fully qualified file name,

36
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.910
which consists of the name of each directory

37
00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.430
in the path to a file plus the file name itself

38
00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:04.670
can be up to 1,023 bytes long.

39
00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.390
The hierarchical file system allows for file names

40
00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:09.390
in mixed case.

41
00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.190
Like Unix, ZOS Unix is case sensitive

42
00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:13.870
to file and directory names.

43
00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.670
So for example, in the same directory,

44
00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.590
the file my file in uppercase is a different file

45
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.110
than my file in lowercase.

46
00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.630
The ZOS Unix shell and utilities

47
00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.550
is the interactive interface for users.

48
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.470
It provides a command interface

49
00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.670
to the ZOS Unix environment.

50
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.310
A user gets access to the shell

51
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.030
by either logging on to TSOE

52
00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.910
or by using the remote login features of TCP IP or login

53
00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.670
by using telnet or SSH.

54
00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.950
If they use TSOE,

55
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:51.550
a command called OMVS creates a shell for the user.

56
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.710
The user then works within the shell environment

57
00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.350
until exiting or temporarily switching back

58
00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.510
to the TSOE environment.

59
00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.950
If the user enters ZOS through the R login

60
00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.750
or telnet capabilities of TCP IP,

61
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.950
the shell environment is automatically created for them

62
00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:10.030
and they have limited access to TSO services.

63
00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.270
The shell is a command interpreter

64
00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.910
which accepts commands defined in XPG 4.2 standard.

65
00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.870
Some commands call programs called utilities.

66
00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.310
The shell user can create named lists

67
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.430
of shell commands called a shell script,

68
00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.910
which becomes a path on your command.

69
00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.830
The shell provides support to invoke the ISP editor

70
00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:35.230
to edit and browse HFS files for the TSOE user.

71
00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.630
For the non-TSOE user,

72
00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.470
the VI editor is supported.

73
00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:46.790
Unix system services are included in the ZOS system.

74
00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:51.070
Support is provided for enhanced ASCII functionality.

75
00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.790
Enhanced ASCII makes it easier

76
00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.110
to port internationalized applications

77
00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.750
developed on or for ASCII platforms to ZOS platforms

78
00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.870
by providing conversion from ASCII to epsidic

79
00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.430
and the other way from epsidic to ASCII.

80
00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.630
There's support to make it easier to shut down

81
00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.110
and restart the Unix system services environment.

82
00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:16.190
And support is also provided to allow access control lists

83
00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.830
or ACLs to be used to control access to Unix files

84
00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:24.230
and directories by individual user IDs and group IDs.

85
00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.910
This support provides greater granularity of access

86
00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.030
than the existing permission bit support.

87
00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:35.510
And the key point here is what distinguishes ZOS Unix

88
00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.950
from other variants is that Unix system services

89
00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.830
are integrated into the ZOS base

90
00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.990
instead of added as middleware

91
00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.430
or shipped as a separate operating system.

92
00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.270
And as a result of that,

93
00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.830
not only can Unix applications be written

94
00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.630
for the IBM Z platform,

95
00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.990
they can also take advantage of the ZOS facilities

96
00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:58.190
and IBM Z server hardware

97
00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:02.990
to obtain enterprise level qualities of service.

98
00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:06.710
The ZOS system includes application enablement services

99
00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:10.030
that provide support for object oriented technology,

100
00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.230
a common runtime environment

101
00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:15.070
and tools for creating new user interfaces

102
00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.150
to traditional ZOS applications.

103
00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.350
Programmers can develop and run traditional ZOS applications,

104
00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.430
Unix applications and even ported windows applications

105
00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:29.710
and Java and web server applications just to name a few.

106
00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.470
A C++ compiler is also provided

107
00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:36.870
to simplify the porting of applications to ZOS.

108
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.150
Another real benefit of running Unix applications on IBM Z

109
00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.110
is that those applications can run

110
00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:47.110
on the system Z integrated information processor or zip.

111
00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:49.910
When configured with general purpose processes

112
00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:52.510
within logical partitions running ZOS,

113
00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:55.510
zips might help increase general purpose

114
00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:58.390
processor productivity and might contribute

115
00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.950
to lowering the overall cost of computing

116
00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:03.710
for ZOS Java technology based applications

117
00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:08.270
and some other Unix based workload like XML and CIM.

118
00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.430
Zips are designed to operate asynchronously

119
00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:13.270
with the general processes to execute Java programming

120
00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:17.070
under control of the IBM Java virtual machine or JVM.

121
00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.830
This can help reduce the demands

122
00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.630
and capability requirements on general purpose processes

123
00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.270
which might then be available

124
00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.670
for reallocation to other IBM Z workloads.

125
00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.950
So that's a wrap of this overview.

126
00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:31.670
We're gonna get into much more detail

127
00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:33.070
in the upcoming modules,

128
00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:37.030
starting with a more thorough introduction to ZOS Unix.
